{% if c == 'mattermost' %}
Mattermost reads `{{ mattermost_config_dir }}/config.json` (via `MM_CONFIG`), not the copy inside the
install directory. We only set the keys we own: SiteURL, listen address, database, file storage,
log and plugin paths, metrics, search and the Calls rtcd URL. Everything else stays editable in the
System Console. Database: `{{ mmd_db_host }}:{{ mattermost_db_port }}/{{ mattermost_db_name }}` as
`{{ mattermost_db_user }}`, sslmode `{{ mmd_db_sslmode }}`{{ ', with reads sent to the replicas on ' ~ mattermost_db_replica_hosts | join(', ') if mattermost_db_replica_hosts else '' }}. Files: {{ 'local disk at `' ~ mattermost_data_dir ~ '`' if mattermost_file_driver == 'local' else 'S3 bucket `' ~ mattermost_s3_bucket ~ '` at `' ~ mattermost_s3_endpoint ~ '`' }}.
Calls: {{ 'rtcd at ' ~ mmd_rtcd_url if mmd_rtcd_url else ('built-in media server on port ' ~ mattermost_calls_port if mmd_calls_integrated else 'not configured') }}.
Search: {{ 'OpenSearch at ' ~ mmd_opensearch_url ~ (', indexing on' if mattermost_search_enable | bool else ', indexing off until a licence is installed') if mmd_opensearch_url else 'database search (no OpenSearch configured)' }}.
{% if mmd_opensearch_ca_src %}
The search CA is trusted system-wide (`/etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/mattermost-implement-opensearch.pem`).
{% endif %}

{% elif c == 'postgresql' %}
Tuned for {{ ansible_facts['memtotal_mb'] }} MB of RAM{{ ', sharing the box with Mattermost' if has.mm else '' }}:
shared_buffers is {{ postgresql_shared_buffers_pct }}% of RAM. max_connections is {{ mmd_postgresql_max_connections }}: {{ mmd_postgresql_app_servers }} app server(s) at {{ mattermost_db_max_open_conns }} connections each{{ ' plus ' ~ postgresql_spare_connections ~ ' spare' if postgresql_max_connections == 'auto' else ' (set by hand)' }}.
Listens on `{{ mmd_postgresql_listen }}`. Passwords use SCRAM-SHA-256.
{{ 'TLS is on with a self-signed certificate in the data directory.' if mmd_postgresql_ssl else 'TLS is off because only local clients connect.' }}
{% if postgresql_replication == 'primary' %}

Streams WAL to the read replicas on {{ postgresql_replica_hosts | join(', ') }}, which log in as
`{{ postgresql_replication_user }}`. Each replica holds a replication slot here, so this server keeps WAL
until the replica has it. `max_slot_wal_keep_size` caps that at {{ mmd_postgresql_slot_wal_cap_mb }} MB
per slot, so a replica that is down can't fill this disk. Past the cap the slot is given up and that
replica has to be rebuilt. Check the slots with
`runuser -u postgres -- psql -c 'SELECT slot_name, active, wal_status FROM pg_replication_slots'`.
{% elif postgresql_replication == 'replica' %}

A read-only copy of `{{ postgresql_primary_host }}:{{ postgresql_port }}`, streaming WAL as
`{{ postgresql_replication_user }}` through the slot `{{ postgresql_replication_slot }}` (settings in
`conf.d/replication.conf`, password in `{{ postgresql_passfile }}`). Logins and the Mattermost database
come from the primary. To rebuild it: `systemctl stop postgresql`, move `{{ postgresql_data_dir }}`
aside and run install.sh. To promote it after
losing the primary: `runuser -u postgres -- pg_ctl promote -D {{ postgresql_data_dir }}`, then point
`mattermost_db_host` at this server.
{% endif %}

{% elif c == 'nginx' %}
{% if mmd_tls_enabled %}
TLS terminates here with the certificate in `{{ tls_install_dir }}`. Protocols: {{ nginx_ssl_protocols }}.

{% for line in mmd_tls_info | default([]) %}
- {{ line }}
{% endfor %}

{% else %}
TLS is not configured yet, so Nginx serves Mattermost over plain HTTP on port 80.
{% endif %}
Upstreams: {{ mmd_nginx_upstreams | join(', ') }}. Static assets are cached in `{{ nginx_cache_dir }}`.

{% elif c == 'rtcd' %}
Mattermost registers with rtcd on first contact (self-registration), which is why only
{{ rtcd_api_sources | join(', ') if rtcd_api_sources else 'localhost' }} can reach the API port.
ICE host override: {{ rtcd_ice_host_override if rtcd_ice_host_override else 'not set' }}.

{% elif c == 'nfs' %}
Exported over NFSv4 on TCP {{ nfs_port }}, read-write to {{ nfs_allowed_clients | join(', ') if nfs_allowed_clients else 'nobody yet' }}{{ ' and read-only to ' ~ nfs_readonly_clients | join(', ') if nfs_readonly_clients else '' }}.
NFSv3, UDP, rpcbind and statd are switched off, so {{ nfs_port }}/tcp is the only port the share needs.
Export options: `{{ mmd_nfs_rw_options | default('not worked out') }}`.
{% if nfs_squash == 'all' %}
Every client write lands as `{{ nfs_owner_user }}` (uid {{ mmd_nfs_uid | default('?') }}, gid {{ mmd_nfs_gid | default('?') }}),
so the `mattermost` user on the app servers doesn't need the same uid as here.
{% else %}
Client uids are kept as they are, so the `mattermost` user on each app server must have uid
{{ mmd_nfs_uid | default('?') }} and gid {{ mmd_nfs_gid | default('?') }}, the same as here. root on a client is still squashed.
{% endif %}

Mount it on each Mattermost server:

```bash
dnf install -y nfs-utils
mkdir -p {{ mattermost_data_dir }}
mount -t nfs4 -o {{ nfs_mount_options }} {{ self_ip }}:{{ nfs_export_dir }} {{ mattermost_data_dir }}
```

and add it to `/etc/fstab` there so it comes back after a reboot:

```text
{{ self_ip }}:{{ nfs_export_dir }}  {{ mattermost_data_dir }}  nfs4  {{ nfs_mount_options }}  0 0
```

Mount it before Mattermost is installed. If an app server already has files in
